        <div id="what-it-backs-up" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What a Power Station Can and Cannot Back Up
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every load in your house is limited by two separate numbers: inverter output, which decides whether something can start and run at all, and battery capacity, which decides for how long. A 6,000W inverter on a 1kWh battery would run a lot of things very briefly. For home backup, capacity usually runs out first, so plan around energy per day, not peak watts.
          </p>

          <p>
            The refrigerator is the easiest win. A modern full-size fridge typically uses 1-2kWh per day, with a startup surge of several hundred watts that every unit in this list handles easily. We cover the details, including freezers and older units, in <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/">can a portable power station run a refrigerator</a>. CPAP machines are even easier: 30-60W without a heated humidifier, or roughly 0.3-0.5kWh a night.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sump pumps are where people underestimate. A 1/3 or 1/2 HP pump draws several hundred watts running and more at startup, and how much energy it uses depends entirely on how often it cycles. In a light rain it may run a few minutes an hour; in a spring thaw with a high water table it can run nearly constantly and drain a 2kWh battery overnight. Our <a href="/sump-pump-battery-backup-vs-generator/">sump pump battery backup vs generator</a> guide covers when a dedicated pump battery is the better answer.
          </p>

          <p>
            A gas furnace needs electricity for its blower, igniter and controls, usually 400-800W while running. That is easy for the inverter but heavy on capacity: in cold weather the blower may run half the time, which can mean 4-8kWh per day. Most furnaces are hardwired, so the safe way to power one is through a transfer switch circuit, not by cutting the cord off and fitting a plug.
          </p>

          <p>
            Well pumps and central air are the hard cases. Most deep well pumps are 240V and pull a big inrush current, so only the 240V units here are candidates, and only if the inverter has the surge headroom. Central AC is technically possible on the largest units with a soft starter, but a 3-ton system pulling around 3,500W drains a 4kWh battery in about an hour. Electric water heaters, ranges, dryers and heat pumps in heating mode are simply not realistic on a portable battery.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Size for kilowatt-hours per day, then check the inverter.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">List what must run, estimate each load's daily energy, add 20% for inverter losses, and compare against usable capacity. Only then check that the inverter can start the biggest motor on the list.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

