        <div id="connecting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Connecting a Power Station to Your House
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are three legitimate ways to get power from a station to the things in your house, and one dangerous way that people keep trying. The simplest is extension cords: plug the fridge, CPAP and router directly into the unit's outlets with properly rated cords. It costs nothing, it is completely safe if the cords are heavy enough, and it is how most people with a 1-2kWh unit should operate. The limitation is that it cannot reach hardwired loads like the furnace or well pump.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second option is a manual transfer switch: an electrician installs a small switch panel beside your main panel, wired to a handful of essential circuits, with a power inlet box on the outside wall. In an outage you plug the station into the inlet and flip each circuit from utility to generator. It reaches hardwired loads, it physically prevents backfeeding, and it is the right answer for most 3-4kWh setups. Our <a href="/best-manual-transfer-switch/">manual transfer switch picks</a> cover the units worth installing, and most power station inlets use the same L14-30 or TT-30 connectors as generators.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third option is a manufacturer smart panel, such as EcoFlow's Smart Home Panel 2 or Anker's home power panel. These replace or sit beside your main panel, switch automatically when the grid drops, and let you prioritize circuits from an app. Transfer is fast enough that clocks and routers often do not notice. They cost more and lock you into one brand, but for the DELTA Pro Ultra and the larger EcoFlow and Anker systems they are what make the battery behave like a standby system.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NEVER DO THIS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Do not backfeed through a dryer outlet with a double-male cord.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A so-called suicide cord energizes your house wiring without disconnecting it from the grid. It can electrocute a line worker down the street, it puts live voltage on the exposed prongs of the plug, and a power station's inverter can be destroyed when utility power returns. Use cords, a transfer switch or a listed panel. Nothing else.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            An interlock kit on your main panel is a fourth, cheaper route that some jurisdictions allow and others do not; it mechanically prevents the main breaker and a generator breaker from being on at the same time. We compare the two in <a href="/interlock-kit-vs-transfer-switch/">interlock kit vs transfer switch</a>. Whichever you choose, have a licensed electrician install it and pull a permit. A transfer switch or interlock installed properly is a one-time cost that makes every future outage easier.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="voltage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. 120V vs 240V: Which Do You Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            North American homes get split-phase power: two 120V legs that combine to 240V. Most outlets, refrigerators, CPAPs, sump pumps and furnace blowers run on 120V, from a single leg. Large appliances such as well pumps, central AC condensers, electric water heaters, dryers and ranges use 240V across both legs. If none of your must-run loads are 240V, a 120V station is fine and you save money.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch shows up at the transfer switch. Most generator transfer switches and inlets are wired for a 240V source, with circuits split across both legs. A 120V-only station feeding a 240V-ready inlet will only power the circuits on one leg, unless the electrician wires the inlet to bridge both legs for 120V-only use and avoids any 240V circuits. That is doable, but tell your electrician which station you own before the work starts.
          </p>

          <p>
            Of our picks, the DELTA Pro 3, F3800 Plus and DELTA Pro Ultra output 240V from a single unit. The Jackery 2000 Plus can reach 240V by pairing two units. The AC200L, DELTA 2 Max and DELTA 2 are 120V only. For houses on a well, that list decides the purchase: no 240V output, no water. For houses on city water with gas heat, it rarely matters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Surge capacity matters as much as voltage for 240V motors. A 1 HP well pump can pull several times its running current for a fraction of a second at startup. Check the pump's nameplate, look up its locked rotor amps, and compare against the station's surge rating, not its continuous rating. If the numbers are close, a soft starter on the pump is cheap insurance. Our <a href="/what-size-generator-runs-a-well-pump/">well pump sizing guide</a> covers the arithmetic.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="expansion" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Expansion Batteries: When They Make Sense
          </h2>

          <p>
            Expansion batteries are the most cost-effective way to add runtime once you own a capable inverter. Instead of buying a second complete station, you add a battery pack that plugs into the first one and shares its inverter, outlets and charging inputs. Every serious home backup unit on this list supports at least one expansion battery, and the big systems support five or six.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case for expansion is strongest when your inverter is already big enough for your loads but your outages last longer than your battery. A DELTA Pro 3 with one extra battery roughly doubles capacity to about 8kWh, which carries a fridge, CPAP and basics for roughly three days without any recharging. An F3800 Plus with two BP3800s lands near 11.5kWh, enough for a winter weekend with the furnace blower running.
          </p>

          <p>
            The case against is cost per kWh at the small end. Expansion batteries for 2kWh units are often priced close enough to the base unit that two full stations make more sense, since a second station also gives you a second inverter and outlets in another room. Run the numbers per kWh before assuming expansion is cheaper.
          </p>

          <p>
            Plan expansion around recharging, too. A 12kWh battery bank is only useful in a long outage if you can refill it, and a single 400W folding panel will not. If you are buying three batteries, budget for a solar array or a generator large enough to make a dent in them. The bigger the bank, the more the recharge plan matters.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="recharging" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Recharging During a Multi-Day Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            A power station is a fuel tank, not an engine. Once the battery is empty, you need another source, and in a multi-day outage that means solar, a gas generator or a car. The best solar generator for home backup is really just a large station with high solar input and enough panels to use it, which is why we weighted solar input heavily in the rankings.
          </p>

          <p>
            Solar math is simple: panel wattage times peak sun hours times about 0.75 for real-world losses. A 800W array in four peak sun hours yields roughly 2.4kWh, which covers a day of fridge, CPAP and basics in summer. In a winter storm with heavy clouds, the same array may deliver a quarter of that. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/">charging a power station with solar</a> covers panel wiring, voltage limits and angles, and our <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">portable solar panel picks</a> cover what to buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            A small inverter generator is the most reliable recharge source, and pairing it with a battery is the setup we recommend most often. Run the generator outside for two or three hours, refill the station at its full AC charging rate, then shut the generator off and run the house silently from the battery overnight. A 2,000W-class inverter generator burns well under a gallon refilling a 2-4kWh battery, compared with burning fuel all night running a fridge that only needs 60W on average.
          </p>

          <p>
            Car charging through a 12V socket is the slowest option, typically 100-120W, so it is a top-up for phones and CPAPs rather than a way to refill a home battery. Never run a car in an attached garage to charge anything. If you are deciding how fast your unit can refill, see <a href="/how-long-does-it-take-to-charge-a-portable-power-station/">how long it takes to charge a power station</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whatever source you use, charge during the day and discharge at night. Solar only produces in daylight, a generator is easier to run while you are awake, and the overnight period is when the fridge, CPAP and furnace still need power while nobody is around to manage loads. Starting every night full is the habit that makes a 4kWh battery feel like a much bigger one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-generator" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Power Station vs Gas or Standby Generator
          </h2>

          <p>
            A gas portable generator delivers far more energy per dollar. A 9,000W dual fuel generator costs roughly what a 2kWh power station costs, and as long as you have fuel it never runs out. A standby generator on natural gas runs automatically for days and covers the whole house, but installed costs commonly run well into five figures. For pure runtime, combustion wins, and our <a href="/best-home-generator-for-power-outages/">home generator picks</a> cover the best options.
          </p>

          <p>
            A power station wins on everything else. It is silent, so it can run overnight without bothering anyone. It produces no carbon monoxide, so it can sit in the kitchen or bedroom; portable generators kill people every storm season when they are run in garages or near windows. It needs no fuel storage, no oil changes and no carburetor cleaning. And it starts instantly, which matters for CPAPs and anything that cannot wait for someone to go outside and pull a cord.
          </p>

          <p>
            The honest answer for most homes is both. A 2-4kWh station handles the quiet hours and the first night of every outage, and a small inverter generator refills it during the day when outages run long. That combination covers multi-day events for a fraction of the cost of a standby system, and the battery handles the hundreds of short outages a year where nobody wants to drag a generator out.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want hands-off whole-home coverage, though, compare a standby generator against a permanently installed battery. Our <a href="/portable-power-station-vs-generator/">portable power station vs generator</a> guide breaks down cost per kWh, and the <a href="/best-whole-house-generator/">whole-house generator picks</a> cover the installed options. A DELTA Pro Ultra with a smart panel competes directly with a mid-size standby generator, without the gas line or annual service.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="keeping-ready" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Keeping It Ready for the Next Outage
          </h2>

          <p>
            The best backup is the one that is actually charged when the power goes out. LiFePO4 batteries hold their charge well, losing only a few percent a month, but a unit that sat untouched in the garage for a year is a gamble. The simplest approach is to leave it plugged into the wall in its pass-through or UPS mode, so it stays full and switches over automatically for anything plugged into it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you store it unplugged, check it every three months and top it off before storm season. Most manufacturers recommend storing at partial charge for long periods, but for a home backup unit the risk of an outage outweighs the small aging benefit; keep it near full during hurricane, ice storm or wildfire season. Keep the firmware current too, since updates have improved charging behavior and fixed app bugs on several of these units.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store it somewhere between roughly 32 and 95 degrees Fahrenheit. LiFePO4 cells should not be charged below freezing, and an unheated garage in a northern winter can block charging until the battery warms. If your garage freezes, keep the station inside and run cords or the inlet cable out to it. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-portable-power-stations-last/">how long power stations last</a> covers what shortens battery life.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do a dry run twice a year. Flip the transfer switch, plug in the station, and run the house on battery for an hour. Confirm the sump pump starts, the furnace blower runs and the fridge compressor kicks on. Label the cords, keep the inlet cable with the station and write the steps on a card taped to the transfer switch, because in a real outage it may be someone else doing it in the dark. For more on the category, start with our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">best portable power station</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying on watts instead of watt-hours</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The big number on the box is usually inverter output, which only tells you what can start. Runtime comes from capacity in watt-hours. A 3,000W unit with 1kWh of storage runs out quickly; a 2,400W unit with 4kWh will get you through the weekend. Size capacity first, then confirm the inverter.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning for summer and getting caught in winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A summer outage needs a fridge and some lights. A winter outage with gas heat adds a furnace blower that can use 4-8kWh a day, more than the fridge and CPAP combined. If you live where winter storms knock out power, size for the cold-weather load profile, not the July one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Backfeeding through a dryer outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A double-male cord into a dryer or range outlet energizes the house wiring without isolating it from the grid. It can kill a line worker, it exposes live prongs, and it can destroy the inverter when the utility returns. Use extension cords, a transfer switch, an interlock kit or a listed smart panel.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a 120V unit for a house on a well</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most deep well pumps are 240V. A 120V-only station cannot run them at all, no matter how much capacity it has, and it cannot power both legs of a typical transfer switch. Check the pump nameplate before buying, and choose a 240V-capable unit if water depends on it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Having no recharge plan</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 4kWh battery buys a day or two of essentials, and then it is empty. Without solar panels, a small generator or both, a three-day outage becomes a two-day outage plus a dark night. Decide before the storm how you will refill the battery and test that the source actually charges it.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size portable power station do I need for home backup?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a refrigerator, CPAP and networking through one night, 1-2kWh is enough. For a full day or a weekend of those essentials, buy 3-4kWh. If you need the furnace blower in winter or a well pump, plan on 4kWh or more plus 240V output, and add expansion batteries or a recharge plan. Size from daily kilowatt-hours, not inverter watts.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a portable power station run my whole house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not in the way a standby generator can. The largest systems, like the DELTA Pro Ultra with several batteries, can run most essential circuits for days, but electric heat, water heaters, ranges and central AC will drain even 30kWh quickly. Realistically, a power station backs up the circuits that matter and leaves the big resistive loads off until the grid returns.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a solar generator the same as a portable power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, in practice. A solar generator is a portable power station sold with solar panels, or marketed around solar charging. There is no engine. The battery and inverter are identical to a station bought on its own. The best solar generator for home backup is a large-capacity station with high solar input paired with enough panels to refill it in a day.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug a power station into a wall outlet to power the house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. Plugging a station into a wall outlet with a double-male cord backfeeds the house wiring, which can electrocute utility workers and damage the station. The safe options are extension cords to individual appliances, a manual transfer switch with an inlet box, an interlock kit where permitted, or a manufacturer smart panel installed by an electrician.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a power station run a refrigerator during a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide usable capacity by the fridge's average draw. A typical full-size fridge averages around 60W over a day, so a 1,024Wh unit runs it about 14 hours, a 2,048Wh unit about 28 hours, and a 4,096Wh unit a bit over two days. Older or larger fridges and frequent door openings shorten that, sometimes considerably.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are portable chargers enough for a power outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For phones and tablets, yes. A 20,000mAh power bank recharges a typical phone several times, and a pair of them covers a family for a short outage. They cannot run a refrigerator, CPAP for long, or a sump pump. If anything beyond phones needs power, a 1kWh-plus power station is the smallest thing that actually helps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy a power station or a gas generator for outages?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                If outages are usually under a day, a power station is quieter, safer and easier. If they regularly last several days and you need high loads, a gas generator delivers far more energy per dollar. The best setup for most homes is both: a station to run the house quietly overnight and a small inverter generator to refill it during the day.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

